---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = "/compare/klaviyo-vs-drip";
const title = "Klaviyo vs Drip for Shopify: predictions or explicit journey logic?";
const desc = "Evaluate Klaviyo and Drip for Shopify replenishment using prediction eligibility, workflow control, order data, SMS and migration effort.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Will Klaviyo predict when every product needs replenishment?",
    "answer": "Do not assume a customer-level prediction is an SKU consumption model. Check eligibility and sample profiles, then compare the proposed timing against product-specific rules and subscription state."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Drip run Shopify order-based workflows?",
    "answer": "Its documented workflow and Shopify integration support that evaluation. Confirm the particular order fields, app embed and external subscription data required by your journey."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I copy workflows during migration?",
    "answer": "Plan to translate and validate the logic. Field names, goal entry behavior, waiting customers and consent handling require explicit mapping; an export alone does not preserve automation behavior."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8"><a class="link" href="/compare">All comparisons</a></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Both candidates deserve an actual workflow evaluation. For a repeat-purchase store, the useful distinction is whether historical predictions change a marketing decision or an operator needs explicit product-specific rules that another person can audit.</p></header>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">

<h2>Separate data eligibility from feature availability</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360020919731">Klaviyo’s predictive analytics guide</a> describes eligibility conditions, including at least 500 purchasing customers and 180 days of order history. A new store cannot infer that predictions will be usable merely because it buys a plan. Review the full current conditions in the account, including recent orders and repeat-purchase history. A prediction is an estimate, not a guaranteed replenishment date for a specific SKU.</p>
<p><a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424702569357-Getting-Started-with-Workflows">Drip’s workflow guide</a> uses triggers, decisions and goals, including Shopify order-based entry. That supports a rule-driven evaluation. It does not prove the editor will be easier for your team or that custom subscription and consumption fields already exist in Shopify.</p>
<h2>Proposed scenario: coffee bags with different reorder intervals</h2>
<p>Suppose a roaster sells small bags, bulk bags and recurring subscriptions. This is a proposed test fixture, not an observed merchant. Define an explicit policy for who should receive a reorder reminder, then evaluate a predicted next-order approach separately from product-rule timing. Use the same controlled customers and orders in both tools.</p>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Question to resolve</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>First small-bag purchase</td><td>What happens when there is too little individual history for a prediction?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bulk bag plus small bag</td><td>Which item governs the reminder, and can the operator explain why?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Active subscription</td><td>Where does subscription state originate, and how does it exclude unnecessary reminders?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reorder during a delay</td><td>Does the existing reminder exit before another message is queued?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Refunded order</td><td>Does refund state change timing or require an additional integration?</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Workflow control: inspect the path, not the canvas screenshot</h2>
<p>Build a purchase-triggered reminder with a subscription exclusion and a reorder exit. In Drip, inspect how <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424695599757-Workflows-Decisions">Decisions</a> split paths and how <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424710499981-Workflows-Goals">Goals</a> move people downstream. Specifically check the setting that permits a goal to act as an entry point; accidentally enabling it could admit people outside the intended purchase path.</p>
<p>In Klaviyo, build the same policy using the available trigger and filters, then separately test the proposed predictive segment. Ask a second operator to change the bulk-bag delay, exclude an unavailable roast and explain the resulting audience. Record mistakes, help requests and time spent. This measures team fit without declaring either builder universally clearer.</p>
<h2>Shopify setup and channel scope</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424695491725-Connect-Your-Shopify-Store">Drip’s Shopify setup</a> includes enabling its app embed. <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/56141874651803">Klaviyo’s Shopify getting-started guide</a> also identifies an app embed for forms and on-site tracking. Check the installed theme, event payloads, customer identity and order history before attributing a missing trigger to the workflow editor.</p>
<p>Drip currently advertises integrated SMS on its <a href="https://www.drip.com/sms">SMS page</a>; treating it as email-only would be misleading. For either candidate, confirm supported destinations, sender setup and consent import in the actual account before adding texts. Email permission alone should not be used as the SMS subscription flag.</p>
<h2>Reporting and total effort</h2>
<p>Request the same report from each trial: entrants, suppressed customers, messages sent, repeat orders, discounts and refunds for the reminder workflow. Align attribution windows before comparing dashboard revenue. Where practical, a later randomized holdout can estimate incremental effect; this page contains no measured lift or merchant outcomes.</p>
<p>Quote current billable profiles or people, send allowances, SMS units, seats and support for the same workload. Include the time spent maintaining external fields, documenting logic and interpreting predictions. Avoid deleting customer history merely to reduce a quote; distinguish suppression, marketing eligibility and records needed for service or analysis.</p>
<h2>Migration is a logic translation</h2>
<p>Export permission evidence, field meanings and active automation membership. Map tags and properties explicitly. Rebuild one journey with sending restricted to owned test addresses; compare entries and exits before transferring ownership. Do not run both recovery senders on the same shoppers. Preserve rollback instructions and agree what happens to people already waiting in an old delay.</p>
<h2>Decision</h2>
<p>Favor Klaviyo when eligible predictive data actually changes the audience or timing and the resulting policy is understandable to the team. Favor Drip when explicit rules and its workflow model pass the operator handoff with less maintenance for your required journey. Catalog size and monthly revenue alone do not settle that choice.</p>
<p>Continue with <a class="link" href="/compare/drip-vs-mailchimp">Drip vs Mailchimp</a> or the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart recovery playbook</a> if campaign migration or purchase exits are the remaining constraint.</p>

</article></div></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Questions before choosing" />
</BaseLayout>
